<!--
  What every acceptance project needs, in one place — `test/` and `examples/` alike,
  since an example is a test in every mechanical respect and differs only in provenance.

  A test directory has no `.csproj` of its own for the build: `test/build-cs.sh`
  synthesises a *stock* one into `obj/`, deliberately without this file, because the
  obligation it checks is that annotated C# compiles as ordinary C# — a project that
  loaded pal-csharp's own analyzer would no longer be checking that.

  These projects are for the editor. They exist so that a C# language server can open a
  test file and show what `palcs` would say about it, live, and they load the analyzer for
  exactly that reason. They are generated by `test/cs-projects.sh`, which the lint runs in
  checking mode, so a directory added to the tree cannot be silently left without one.

  One project per directory is not a stylistic choice: the tests reuse type names across
  directories on purpose (there is more than one `Counter`), so a single project spanning
  them would not compile.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <!-- A test declares fields it never reads; that is the point of some of them. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0169;CS0414;CS0649</NoWarn>
    <PalRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..</PalRoot>
    <PalAnalyzerDir>$(PalRoot)/src/Pal.Analyzers/bin/$(Configuration)/netstandard2.0</PalAnalyzerDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="$(PalRoot)/src/Pal.Annotations/Pal.Annotations.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The analyzer is referenced as a project so that opening a test file builds it first,
    and `ReferenceOutputAssembly` is off because a test must not link against the
    translator — it only wants to be looked at by it. The other two assemblies are what
    the analyzer loads at run time, and an `Analyzer` item is the only way to put them in
    its load context; a `ProjectReference` would put them on the compile line instead,
    which is the opposite of what is wanted.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="$(PalRoot)/src/Pal.Analyzers/Pal.Analyzers.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    <Analyzer Include="$(PalAnalyzerDir)/Pal.Compiler.dll" />
    <Analyzer Include="$(PalAnalyzerDir)/Pal.Annotations.dll" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    What Pulse said, last time it was asked. `make` writes this; the analyzer reads it and
    puts each message on the C# line it is about.

    `AdditionalFiles` rather than the analyzer opening the file itself: an analyzer that
    does its own I/O is reading something Roslyn does not know it depends on, so the
    result is cached against a key that does not include it and the squiggles go stale.
    Listed this way, re-running `make` is enough to update them.

    `Condition` on existence, because a directory that has never been verified has no
    results, and that is a normal state rather than a missing file.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AdditionalFiles Include="out/verification.txt" Condition="Exists('out/verification.txt')" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
